<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helen of Troy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Helen of Sparta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Greek mythology, Helen was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and Leda and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. Her abduction or flight with the Trojan prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> caused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, told in the Homeric epics. She was worshipped as a goddess in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, notably at the Menelaion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therapne">Therapne</a> near Sparta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helen-of-troy.jpg" alt="Helen of Troy" width="320" height="656" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helen_of_Sparta_boards_a_ship_for_Troy_fresco_from_the_House_of_the_Tragic_Poet_in_Pompeii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
